  <article class="post" lang="en">
    <h2>The Daily Note: Why a Date in the Filename Keeps Your System Alive</h2>
    <p class="meta">04. August 2026 · Method · English</p>
    <p>Most note systems do not fail while you build them — they fail while you
    <strong>use</strong> them daily. The structure is exciting for two weeks, then it
    becomes just another chore. The simplest counter-move is also the most unassuming
    part of the Mini-Vault: the <strong>daily note</strong>. One note per day, named
    after the date — that is the whole rule, and exactly that is its power.</p>
    <p><strong>The filename is the date — and with it, every decision disappears.</strong>
    No thinking about which folder something belongs to, no wrestling for the right
    title: today is <em>2026-08-04</em>, so the note is called <em>2026-08-04.md</em>.
    One decision less per day means you actually stick with it — and sticking with it
    gives you a journal almost for free: in <code>06_Daily</code> the notes lie side by
    side in chronological order, and a year later you flip through your own year
    without having searched for a single second.</p>
    <p><strong>Seven minutes in the morning are enough.</strong> Create the daily note,
    write down three goals (the most important one first), and add the focus: the one
    thing you will work on today if nothing else works out. The template has a field
    for exactly that — <strong>“the sentence that saves the day when everything goes
    sideways.”</strong> Nothing more needs to happen in the morning; the system carries
    the rest.</p>
    <p><strong>In the evening, the circle closes.</strong> Process the inbox (the rule
    from the first post of this series), then finish the daily note with three short
    lines: <strong>good today</strong> · <strong>learned today</strong> ·
    <strong>important tomorrow</strong>. The last point is the quiet engine: “important
    tomorrow” moves straight into the fresh daily note the next morning — every note
    points to the next one, and the rhythm carries itself. That is how the whole
    system costs seventeen minutes a day, and nothing gets lost.</p>
    <p><strong>The daily note is the control center of your network.</strong> It links
    the projects you work on today (project note) and the ideas that cross your mind
    on the way (inbox → permanent note). The “always link” rule applies here too: a
    daily note without a single link is a lost day. Obsidian's backlink view later
    shows you at a glance what happened on that date — which project was running,
    which idea was born, what was on your mind. That is exactly the difference
    between a filing system and a second brain: it remembers even when you do not.</p>
    <p>Want to try it right away? The free
    <a href="second-brain-in-10-minuten-v0.2.zip" download>Mini-Vault</a> comes with the
    daily note template ready to use — the three goals, the focus field and the evening
    routine included. It is up and running in about ten minutes.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="second-brain-in-10-minuten-v0.2.zip" download>Download the free Mini-Vault (ZIP)</a>
