  <article class="post" lang="en">
    <h2>The 17-Minute Rule: How a Fixed Evening Routine Keeps Your Note System Alive</h2>
    <p class="meta">04. August 2026 · Method · English</p>
    <p>Most note systems do not die from the wrong structure but from a missing rhythm:
    collecting starts with enthusiasm, after two weeks the inbox stays full, after a month
    nobody opens the vault anymore. The counter-movement is a fixed, <strong>short</strong>
    routine — not a time-management system, but a standing appointment with yourself.</p>
    <p><strong>The rule in one sentence:</strong> Every evening, 17 minutes — no more, no
    less — for three fixed steps.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1. Empty the inbox (7 minutes):</strong> Everything that landed in the
      inbox during the day gets decided: delete it, file it, or turn it into its own note.
      One idea per note, link it briefly, put it away.</li>
      <li><strong>2. Write the daily note (5 minutes):</strong> What mattered today? What
      is still open? The daily note is not a diary — it is the red thread of your system
      and makes visible that the work is actually moving forward.</li>
      <li><strong>3. Plan tomorrow (5 minutes):</strong> Write the 1–3 most important
      things for tomorrow into the next daily note. That way the next day does not start
      in an empty space but with a clear first step.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Why exactly 17 minutes?</strong> Short enough to be doable every day — even
    on bad days. Long enough to actually get the basket empty. The fixed duration removes
    the decision (<em>“when do I stop?”</em>) and turns an annoying chore into a closed,
    small unit. A timer helps: if you are still in the middle of a note after 17 minutes,
    you may continue — but the basket is already emptied.</p>
    <p><strong>Why this holds:</strong> Systems fail on friction. A fixed routine at the
    same time, with the same duration and the same steps brings friction down to almost
    zero — after two weeks it runs without willpower. And an empty inbox in the morning
    is the most visible success a note system has to offer.</p>
    <p>Want to try it right away? The free
    <a href="second-brain-in-10-minuten-v0.2.zip" download>Mini-Vault</a> is built exactly
    for this routine — daily note template, 3 folders, 6 templates (EN + DE), and a
    one-page guide with the 17-minute rule. It is up and running in about ten minutes.</p>
    <a class="btn" href="second-brain-in-10-minuten-v0.2.zip" download>Download the free Mini-Vault (ZIP)</a>
