  <article class="post" lang="en">
    <h2>The Inbox Rule: Why 3 Folders Are Enough to Keep Your Note System from Collapsing</h2>
    <p class="meta">04. August 2026 · Method · English</p>
    <p>Most note systems fail not on the idea but on the structure: too many folders, too
    many rules, too much maintenance. After two weeks the collection is there — and nobody
    dares to touch it anymore. The counter-movement is radically simple: <strong>everything
    lands in an inbox first</strong>, and just three folders handle the rest.</p>
    <p><strong>The 3-folder rule:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>00_Inbox</code> — the entry basket. Every note, every thought, every
      forwarded link lands here. No sorting while capturing — sorting only costs focus.</li>
      <li><code>06_Daily</code> — the daily notes. This is where the red thread is spun:
      what mattered today, what is coming tomorrow. Five minutes in the evening are enough.</li>
      <li><code>07_Templates</code> — the templates. Daily note, project note, permanent
      note. They turn a loose note into a reusable structure without you having to think it
      through from scratch every time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Everything else is optional. Topic hubs, zettelkasten logic, complex tagging — all of
    that can be added later, once your system is <em>running</em>. In the beginning only one
    thing counts: that you create another note tomorrow without being afraid of your own
    system.</p>
    <p><strong>Why this works:</strong> The inbox lowers the barrier to entry to zero
    (capturing is always allowed), and a fixed processing time — for example 17 minutes a
    day — keeps the basket from overflowing. That is no coincidence: it is the same
    mechanism as an email inbox. What has a clear one-way path stays empty and therefore
    usable.</p>
    <p>Want to try it right away? The free
    <a href="second-brain-in-10-minuten-v0.2.zip" download>Mini-Vault</a> is built exactly
    on this rule — 3 folders, 6 templates (EN + DE), a one-page guide. It is up and running
    in about ten minutes.</p>
